{
  "requirement": "`trust_but_verify::agent::Tool` supplies the name and description shown to the\nmodel. `execute` can await work using the context of the current criterion.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Tool<D: Domain, A, R>: Send + Sync {\n    fn name(&self) -> &str;\n    fn description(&self) -> &str;\n    fn execute(&self, cx: &ValidationContext<'_, D>, args: A,)\n        -> impl Future<Output = R> + Send;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The requirement prescribes a trait signature: `Tool<D: Domain, A, R>: Send + Sync` with `name`, `description` and `execute(&self, cx: &ValidationContext<'_, D>, args: A) -> impl Future<Output = R> + Send`. These clauses are structural. For example, an alternative that returns a boxed future or takes `cx` by value would behave the same at runtime but would not match the stated signature. The sole root, `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/config.rs#trait[Tool]`, is marked {impl} and declares exactly that signature. It has the generics, the Send + Sync supertraits, both `&str` accessors, and the async-capable `execute` taking the validation context. No other structural obligation exists. Which name and description the model sees, and what `execute` awaits, are behavioral and are covered by tests.\n\nbehavioral: The subject `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.Tool` has two observable obligations. The reviewed test is `mod[tests]/fn[registered_tools_use_the_current_context_and_ordinary_node_ids]` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs`, and it covers both.\n\n1. **Name and description shown to the model.**\n   - The test asserts that the first four requests list the tools `[read_node, outgoing_edges, get_next_page, submit_result, lookup_requirements, inspect]`. The last two names come from `Tool::name`.\n   - A second domain registers `Lookup(\"other\")`. The test asserts that request 4 shows `other` and omits `inspect` and `lookup_requirements`, so the name comes from the tool instance.\n   - It asserts `lookup[\"description\"]` equals \"Find requirement node IDs matching a name glob.\", which is the `description()` value.\n   - `Inspect`'s description is not asserted. It goes through the same path, so this is not a separate behavior.\n\n2. **`execute` uses the current criterion's context.**\n   - `Lookup::execute` calls `cx.graph()` and records the pattern. The test asserts `lookup_calls == [\"requirements.sdk.*\"]`, and the returned node IDs `[1, 2]` are checked in the tool output.\n   - `Inspect::execute` calls `cx.read` and `cx.outgoing_edges`. The test asserts that `cx.dependencies()` contains exactly the `Node` and `OutgoingEdges(..., \"related\")` dependencies for the outline node. This shows the tool ran against the current context. The inspect output string is also asserted.\n   - The `execute` signature is an `async` future, and the test runs it through the agent loop with results fed back to the model. The tools never await real work. \"Can await work\" is a capability, and the signature itself enforces it. It is not a separate behavior to test.\n\nNo obligation of `Tool` is left untested.",
  "references": "0e84020ca55a9be160c76db73253af448f9720ca072818d2b9f4a3a3548c51e4",
